FIXED (mechanism qualified on silicon at the full 80-layer shape, both arms, one process);
three-fresh-process gate runs IN FLIGHT

--- c-defects attempt 6, 2026-08-31 ---

D-C15: A CLOSED GALAXY MODEL DOES NOT RETURN ITS LAYER WEIGHTS (DRAM).

This is the defect behind the Llama half of D-C7's gate. D-C7 is named for L1 and its L1
half is fixed and qualified (see D-C7.status, D-C14.status). What stopped
test_llama_two_paged_pools_agree_and_a_contiguous_cache_is_unreachable was a different
resource entirely.

MEASURED, three fresh processes, byte-identical, full 80-layer shape, commit faec6e59938
(logs/t4_llama_two_pools_r1.log, t5_..r2, t6_..r3):
  TT_FATAL: Out of Memory: Not enough space to allocate 2297856 B DRAM buffer across 11
  banks, where each bank needs to store 208896 B, but bank size is 1070773184 B
  (allocated: 1070239264 B, free: 533920 B, largest free block: 99712 B)
  raised at layer53_wqkv_ring - the SECOND model's ring-weight pass, 66% through, with the
  first model already closed, deleted and gc.collect()ed. Each run then hung in teardown
  until the 3600 s bound (rc=124).

CAPACITY WAS REFUTED, not assumed away (logs/x1_llama_pool4096_only_full.log):
  the two-pools case is asymmetric - arm 1 is max_seq_len=2048 with the default 2048-block
  pool, arm 2 is max_seq_len=4096 with an explicit 4096-block pool, so arm 2's KV is twice
  arm 1's. Built ALONE at the full 80-layer shape, arm 2 builds, prefills, decodes and
  closes: 1 passed in 239 s. The second model fits. What does not fit is the second model
  plus the first model's weights.

RETENTION WAS MEASURED (same log):
  after close(), del and gc.collect() -
    398 617 984 B per DRAM bank still allocated, 961 blocks
    240 of 240 prefetcher-registered weights still live
  which is 37% of the 1 070 773 184 B bank. At a four-layer subset
  (logs/w2_llama_dram_probe_l4.log): 19 931 264 B, 49 blocks, 12 of 12.
  The residual histogram names the owner:
    650624 x240 = 80 layers x 3   (w1 / w2 / w3)
    696320 x160, 731136 x80, 278528 x80, 232832 x80, 208896 x80, 186048 x80  (attention;
      208896 is exactly the size of the wqkv_ring buffer the OOM died on)
    384 x161 = two RMS norms per layer plus the one final norm

THE CAUSE: close() released the embedding, the LM head, the rotary setup, sampling and the
column selector, and left attention and MLP alone. MLP2D had NO release and NO close at
all, and <Model>TransformerBlock2D.close() called only self.attention.close(), which
releases intermediates and runtime tensors and never weights. A LazyWeight memoizes its
device tensor in _value, so the weights survive for as long as any caller holds the model -
a runner still bound in an enclosing frame, an executor kept for its metrics. That is what
the two-pools test does and what a serving system does by construction.

FIX, commit 299440bb276 (six files, zero llm_runtime lines, zero *_1d.py lines):
  lazy_weight.release_device_weights   deallocate each distinct materialized weight once,
      clear its memo; dedupe on the LazyWeight AND on its _value, because Attention2D
      resolves prefill_wqkv = resolved.prefill_wqkv or wqkv
  RMSNorm2D.release, MLP2D.release, Attention2D.release
  <Block>.release_weights, called per layer from each model's close()
It is the pattern Embedding2D.release, LMHead2D.release, RotarySetup2D.release and
Sampling2D.release already use. Attention, MLP and RMS norm were the three weight-owning
2D modules that lacked it.

ORDERING IS THE MODEL'S, NOT THE MODULE'S: the release runs AFTER Prefetcher2D.cleanup(),
because the attention decode weights are registered with the prefetcher and freeing one
while a prefetch can still read it is a use-after-free - which on this mesh means a
TT_FATAL inside a multi-subdevice program and an un-drainable teardown, exactly the failure
mode t4-t6 spent three hours in. For the same reason a model that BORROWS shared resources
does not release; it does not control when the prefetch stops. That leaves a
borrowed-resources model's weights resident, recorded rather than assumed away -
owns_shared_resources is True for every model this tree builds.

MEASURED AFTER THE FIX, same probe, same shapes, commit 299440bb276:
  4 layers   (logs/z4_probe_l4_afterfix.log,  72.81 s, 1 passed)
      residue 19 931 264 B -> 0 B, 49 blocks -> 0, 12 of 12 -> 0 of 12
  80 layers, BOTH arms, ONE process
             (logs/z5_probe_full_afterfix.log, 626.11 s, 1 passed)
      0-default-2048-closed   DRAM=0    0 of 240 weights live   residual n=0 bytes=0
      1-explicit-4096-built   DRAM=170 325 056  <- from an EMPTY bank
      1-explicit-4096-closed  DRAM=0    0 of 240 weights live
      zero "Out of Memory" lines in 5 914 lines of log

TESTS:
  host   models/common/tests/modules/test_lazy_weight_release.py - 6 passed; covers both
         aliasing cases and the partial-failure path. Without the primitive it does not
         import.
  device test_<model>_a_closed_model_returns_its_device_weights (commit d2d6c424030), in
         both models' step-7 coverage files: build one model, use it, close it, assert the
         DRAM allocator is back to baseline with handle and runner STILL BOUND - close()
         alone must be sufficient. Threshold zero on principle and zero in measurement.

NOTHING WAS RELAXED. No threshold was moved, no test was deleted or xfailed.

STILL OWED at the time of writing: the committed gate test at three fresh processes on both
models (q13 z6/z7, q13b z8-z11), and the regression set in q14.
